00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.560
PROFESSOR: So how many of you
have exposure or have taken

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.110
any heat transfer classes here?

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.830
OK, it's about half of you.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:49.500
Cool so can you start by
telling me what heat is?

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.054
It's just like pretty basic.

00:01:01.054 --> 00:01:02.710
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:03.700
PROFESSOR: Yeah, OK.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:04.659
It's a form of energy.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.088
What are the units of that?

00:01:13.088 --> 00:01:14.823
AUDIENCE: Joules.

00:01:14.823 --> 00:01:15.490
PROFESSOR: Cool.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.100
And what about heat transfer?

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:30.800
What are-- I guess it's
pretty self-explanatory.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.925
It's just transferring heat.

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:34.425
What are the units
of heat transfer?

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:48.940
Generally, you're trying to
figure out how much energy is

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.110
being transferred per second.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.740
So you're going to have a
joule per second or watts.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:54.240
Yeah.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:06.470
So what are three major modes
of heat transfer as well?

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:07.512
How much do you remember?

00:02:07.512 --> 00:02:09.512
AUDIENCE: Conduction,
convection, and radiation.

00:02:09.512 --> 00:02:10.409
PROFESSOR: Awesome.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:35.160
And for people who haven't
seen this material before,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.720
tell me if I'm going
through it too quickly when

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.030
I started talking about it.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:53.865
But can someone--

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.690
OK, can people explain what the
difference is between the three

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.115
types of heat transfer?

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:11.528
What?

00:03:11.528 --> 00:03:15.855
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHS] I said I'm
giving someone else a shot.

00:03:15.855 --> 00:03:16.480
PROFESSOR: Yes.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.480
AUDIENCE: Conduction is
just through direct contact.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:20.320
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.495
AUDIENCE: Convention
is basically

00:03:35.495 --> 00:03:38.613
conduction except through
a fluid that's moving.

00:03:38.613 --> 00:03:39.280
PROFESSOR: Cool.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.097
That's not how you spell it.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:57.310
And radiation?

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:05.444
Yeah.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:06.527
AUDIENCE: Photon emission.

00:04:13.847 --> 00:04:15.680
PROFESSOR: I think it's
just more generally.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.994
It's just heating through
electromagnetic waves.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.152
But yeah, so that's
kind of the three sort

00:04:25.152 --> 00:04:28.460
of big modes of heat transfer.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.680
We'll be talking
about convection

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.780
and conduction a
lot more later when

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.660
we're doing, say, cooking
and stoves because that plays

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.560
into that a lot more, like, when
you're putting a pot on a fire.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.910
When we're dealing
with solar stuff,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.100
we're going to be dealing
mostly with radiation.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.740
But while we're on
the topic of heat

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:55.910
or while we're on the
topic of heat transfer,

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:58.490
I might as well give you the
equations for the other two,

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.320
but we'll start by
talking about radiation.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:11.860
So when we're dealing
with radiation, we're--

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.223
cool.

00:05:17.223 --> 00:05:18.640
When we're dealing
with radiation,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.910
we're thinking of
objects giving off energy

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:26.500
in electromagnetic waves.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.492
The only thing
that really matters

00:05:28.492 --> 00:05:30.700
when you're dealing with
this is what the temperature

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.830
of the object is.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:35.530
So you can approximate
things as--

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:45.300
or there's an
equation for it, which

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.510
is the heat transfer
due to radiation

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:52.710
is the Stefan-Boltzmann
constant times the area

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.860
of the surface area of the
object times the temperature

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:00.690
to the fourth, which is an
important equation to know.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:05.490
For convective heat transfer,
you have a different equation.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.973
But again, this
isn't that relevant.

00:06:08.973 --> 00:06:10.890
So I'm going to move on
to talking to you guys

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:32.380
about different types of
solar technologies now.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:37.380
So the first one we are
going to talk about is SODIS.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.640
Do you have any--

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:39.720
OK, so you have SODIS.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.730
You have solar thermal.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.490
So solar thermal is when--

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.240
there are different
types of technologies.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:51.840
It's where you're using heat
from the sunlight directly.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.580
So you're using solar
radiation to heat things.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:58.020
And there are three
different sorts of big ways

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:58.700
you can do this.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:00.390
You can use it for
cooking and drying.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.250
You can use it for
heating of water,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.200
and you can use it for
generating electricity.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:09.090
And you also have photovoltaics,
which we worked with last time

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.710
for generating electricity.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:20.540
So SODIS, the way SODIS works
is you fill bottles with water,

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:21.770
and you close the cap.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:25.340
Then you leave these
bottles out on a rooftop,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.950
and you expose them to
sunlight for about six hours.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:32.210
And you can store--
like, the UV light

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.910
from the sun plus
the heat from the sun

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:40.050
also kills the pathogens and
bacteria inside the water.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.350
If you remember-- do you
guys remember from last week

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.460
when we were playing
with the bags?

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.130
There was a SODIS bag.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:50.870
Does that sound familiar at
all with the plastic sealer?

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:51.555
Yeah.

00:07:51.555 --> 00:07:52.430
Why are you laughing?

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:57.320
AUDIENCE: Because we spent like
two hours trying to make one.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:03.230
PROFESSOR: Yeah, so SODIS
is a really cool technology.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:04.670
It's an example
of how you can use

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:07.530
solar energy to purify water.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:10.310
There are other ways you can
use solar energy to purify water

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:13.865
too, but this is supposed to
be one of the better ones.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.750
So I guess we kind of
jumped ahead by showing you

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.030
a really good SODIS
technology last week

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:29.180
before talking to you about
SODIS as a technology.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.460
But in most of the
developing world,

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:34.520
the companies that are
promoting SODIS, what they do

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:39.830
is they tell people to use
two liter bottles, which

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:41.960
are available in a lot
of the developing world,

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.540
and fill them with
water and leave them out

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:46.640
on your rooftops.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.830
Can you guys think of
any ideas or any reasons

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:55.600
why this is not a good idea
or what isn't the best--

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:57.470
OK, why this isn't
the best solution

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.930
to using this technology
or best implementation

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:03.530
of this technology and why,
say, a bag would be better?

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:10.310
So what are some of the
problems with a plastic bottle?

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:13.880
So you have this plastic
bottle, looks kind of like that.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:19.280
Yeah.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:22.411
AUDIENCE: We don't have the
perfect control [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:22.411 --> 00:09:27.430
the bottle might topple over,
fall off, [INAUDIBLE] sit,

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:34.160
whereas the bag [INAUDIBLE]
bag was more just floppy.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.290
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
I actually haven't

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:40.910
seen that particular
issue before,

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:42.260
but that's a good point.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.500
What about in terms
of transportation?

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:53.300
How easy is it to get a plastic
bottle that's shaped like this?

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:58.220
If I wanted to provide an
entire community with these,

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:01.230
how would I send it to them?

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.638
I would have to ship a giant
container with two liter

00:10:03.638 --> 00:10:05.930
bottles, and they don't really
pack well at all, right?

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:09.140
You need to fill like a
crate of two liter bottles.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:12.380
And it just seems really
silly to ship a container

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.660
of empty water bottles.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.730
In places where they
have two liter bottles,

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.167
it's a really good
technology because, I mean,

00:10:19.167 --> 00:10:21.500
if they're just taking existing
things that they already

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:25.370
have and they're using them,
repurposing them for this,

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:26.060
then it's great.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:30.920
But otherwise, it doesn't
seem to make that much sense

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:32.110
to try to fly them in.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:33.860
And that's what some
of the companies that

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.990
were really promoting SODIS
heavily in the beginning

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:36.490
were doing.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.590
They were trying to ship
two liter bottles in bulk

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:45.770
to communities to
get them to use them.

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:48.050
Can you guys think of
any other disadvantages

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:53.390
to using these water bottles?

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:57.410
What about-- so how does--

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:58.970
OK, maybe I'll go
a little bit more

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:00.990
into how the technology works.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:06.590
So you have your water
bottle like this, I guess.

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:07.775
That's not how it looks.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.770
With your tapped water bottle,
it's filled with water.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.930
Like, this is all water.

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:29.420
So what happens is you have
sunlight coming from here.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:30.600
It's heating up the water.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:31.970
It heat-- yes.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:43.922
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] also the
surface area of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:56.740
So I guess the water on the
bottom half of the bottle

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:58.550
will get [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:09.270
PROFESSOR: Yeah, so because
water and your plastic both

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:14.220
absorb some of the
sunlight, you have

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.200
a lot of it being
absorbed by this fluid.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:25.440
And what they say is if this
depth is more than 4 inches,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:32.478
that SODIS's effectiveness
is dropped by maybe 50%

00:12:32.478 --> 00:12:33.520
I think is what they say.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:46.500
So yeah, so that's
another problem.

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.860
I think a two liter bottle
is about the right size.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.260
So still like in the
range of the right size.

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:54.900
So you're not going
to have over--

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.930
or you're not going to have
more than four inches there.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:02.640
But that's definitely something
to take into consideration.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:07.600
What about place-- so
what about the cap?

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.885
Can you guys think of why
the cap might be a problem?

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:14.457
AUDIENCE: It's not transparent.

00:13:14.457 --> 00:13:21.940
So if you have any water
[INAUDIBLE] if you're

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:24.187
pouring water out [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:24.187 --> 00:13:25.770
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
that's exactly, like,

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:31.110
one of the biggest problems with
SODIS with using a water bottle

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.350
is just because the region in
this area where the cap is just

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.360
doesn't--

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:38.250
I mean, it's opaque plastic.

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:40.440
So your UV rays are
getting-- or most of them

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.540
are getting blocked
by the plastic.

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:46.950
And so your bacteria don't
die necessarily over there.

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:51.755
So that's why the bag that
Shawn Frayne came up with,

00:13:51.755 --> 00:13:53.880
the SODIS bag that you guys
saw the other time that

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:56.250
used two one way valves,
works a lot better

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:58.320
because the entire
thing is transparent.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:02.610
And there was a
lot of work people

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:05.370
are trying to do
trying to redesign this

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.870
into a better system.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:12.000
So they looked at the problems
with the transportation

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.430
with the two liter
bottle, and then they

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:16.170
came up with
another bag that was

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:20.970
kind of like just a bag
that had a handle here.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:26.940
And then it had the same sort
of twist-on cap over here

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:32.250
that a two liter
bottle does, like that.

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:35.940
And then they made
the back of the bag

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:39.130
black so that it
would heat up more,

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.130
which is a pretty cool design.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:45.360
But then we still
had problems with--

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:52.470
well, for one, having
expensive molded parts

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:54.960
is also really
expensive because--

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.270
and you can't really
fabricate that everywhere.

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.760
Like, you can't
go into a village

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:03.000
and try to create something
that looks like this or even

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.420
something that looks like this.

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:06.870
Whereas if you have a
simple plastic sealer,

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:11.490
you could make one of the SODIS
bags that we were working on

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.220
or that we saw last week.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.310
Has anyone not seen the
SODIS bags from last week

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:20.580
or have no idea what
I'm talking about?

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:22.630
OK.

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.795
And how many of you have
heard of SODIS before?

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:30.310
OK.

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:33.400
So yeah, so this design sort
of went from this iteration

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:34.450
to this iteration.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:39.380
And then Shawn Frayne came along
and worked on it a little bit

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:41.920
and came up with the
next iteration of--

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:44.650
or came up with his technology,
which is like really, really

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:48.040
good technology.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:49.540
In terms of usage,
because I want

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.670
you guys to start thinking
about these technologies

00:15:51.670 --> 00:15:53.320
and how you would
use them too, what's

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:56.922
another problem with this?

00:15:56.922 --> 00:15:58.130
You're supposed to take this.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:02.200
You're supposed to leave it
out in the sun for six hours,

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:05.320
and then it--

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:06.787
or six hours to
two days, depending

00:16:06.787 --> 00:16:07.870
on the weather conditions.

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:09.400
And then it's supposed
to clean your water,

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:10.733
and you're supposed to drink it.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:14.630
Yeah.

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:16.463
AUDIENCE: I have a
question on how it works.

00:16:16.463 --> 00:16:18.930
What is it-- a chemical
inside the water to purify it?

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:19.513
PROFESSOR: No.

00:16:19.513 --> 00:16:21.160
No, you just take water, right?

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:24.160
And you leave it out in the sun,
and the UV rays from the sun

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:25.600
kill the bacteria.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.030
It breaks down the bacteria.

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:28.313
AUDIENCE: So any container?

00:16:28.313 --> 00:16:29.980
PROFESSOR: Any container
as long as it's

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:33.670
clear and UV-transparent
or translucent.

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:35.600
AUDIENCE: What does
SODIS stand for?

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:37.472
PROFESSOR: Solar disinfection.

00:16:37.472 --> 00:16:38.680
I should have explained that.

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:39.597
Yeah, sorry, you guys.

00:16:39.597 --> 00:16:41.980
SODIS stands for
solar disinfection.

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:44.945
So you're purifying water
just using sunlight.

00:16:44.945 --> 00:16:46.362
AUDIENCE: But is
there any company

00:16:46.362 --> 00:16:48.570
that's going to market this?

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:49.960
PROFESSOR: It's a technique.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:50.140
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK.

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:50.593
AUDIENCE: It's not a product.

00:16:50.593 --> 00:16:51.952
Yeah, it's just a technique.

00:16:51.952 --> 00:16:53.700
[INAUDIBLE]

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.450
AUDIENCE: So then--
like, I know that BPAs

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:58.540
make water bottles are newest
hype because of all the toxins

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:00.920
that plastic releases
when it's in extreme heat,

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:03.880
like in extreme [INAUDIBLE]
or extreme [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:04.380
temperature.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:07.250
Is that part of the
[INAUDIBLE] consideration?

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.954
The idea is that any
[INAUDIBLE] water

00:17:09.954 --> 00:17:11.079
can be used for it exactly?

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:18.810
PROFESSOR: So generally,
leaching from plastic,

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:23.130
like, it's a problem that's on
a much smaller scale than having

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:24.520
unsafe drinking water.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:27.810
And so if you had to choose
the trade-off between having

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:31.110
some plastic potentially
leach into your water

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:34.980
and drink it and en
like, dozens of years,

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:40.110
that might cause some health
problems versus having

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:44.190
unsafe drinking water,
like, you would definitely

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:45.570
choose unsafe--
or you definitely

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:48.660
choose the using SODIS
to drink your water.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:52.200
But yeah, that is one of the
bigger concerns with SODIS.

00:17:58.795 --> 00:17:59.940
Cool.

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:02.385
Are there any other
questions about SODIS?

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:12.115
All right, I'm going to
go into solar drying.

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:17.140
So solar drying is
another technology

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:21.280
that is used in a lot
of places in the world.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:27.730
And the idea is basically that
you take fruit or whatever,

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:30.950
and you drive them so that
you can preserve them.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:32.770
And the thing is,
in a lot of places,

00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:36.280
you'll have farmers who
are growing something,

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:39.680
and they'll all have crops
that grow at the same time.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.350
And so they'll harvest,
and then they'll

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:42.640
go take them to the market.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:44.260
And because there's
an overabundance

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:46.720
of these fruits or
something, people

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:48.730
aren't getting their peak.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:51.070
Like, they're not
getting as much money

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:59.320
as they can for their
fruits because there's way

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:02.410
too much of-- like, there's
way more supply than there's

00:19:02.410 --> 00:19:03.260
a demand for it.

00:19:06.352 --> 00:19:07.810
And they don't have
a very good way

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.050
of preserving food otherwise.

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:11.230
So they can't
refrigerate these fruits.

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:13.840
So if they don't keep
them refrigerated,

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:15.220
then they're just
going to spoil.

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:16.870
So it's either
sell at a low cost

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:19.550
or don't get anything for them.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:23.630
And so their food preservation
is a pretty big deal as well.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:27.580
And so you can use solar
energy to also dry fruits.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:32.180
And this is a project
called Fruits of the Nile

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:37.460
that works in Uganda,
where they do that.

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:39.470
And then I'm going
to go to talking

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:43.340
about solar thermal
panels for water heating.

00:19:45.622 --> 00:19:47.330
I don't know if you
guys have seen these.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:52.220
These are taken from
the US, these pictures.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:57.110
But do any of you have
exposure to solar thermal?

00:19:57.110 --> 00:20:01.950
Yeah, so what you're basically
doing here is you have--

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.340
well, there are two
types of systems which

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:10.340
I guess are on the next slide.

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:13.372
So you have two different
types of systems.

00:20:13.372 --> 00:20:15.080
But this is a little
bit more complicated

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:16.040
than it needs to be.

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:19.280
So basically, the idea is
you're taking in sunlight.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:23.700
And using the sunlight, you're
heating water or some of fluid.

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:26.390
And so you have sort of
this container that's

00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:29.240
shaped like this,
or you have tubes

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:31.490
that have water or some
container or something running

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:32.420
it through it.

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:35.780
Sunlight hits this
container, heats up

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:37.250
the water inside these tubes.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:45.110
And then you store the
hot water in a tank.

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:48.260
So that's a direct
solar thermal system.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:50.900
There's also indirect
solar thermal systems

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:57.560
where instead of having your
water being heated directly,

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:02.210
you have a heat exchanger here.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:05.820
And then you have some of fluid
that goes through the system.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:09.240
And then you have your
reservoir or something.

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:14.288
And what's hitting the water
is just like hot fluid-- like,

00:21:14.288 --> 00:21:15.080
the heat exchanger.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:16.520
So it's heating the water.

00:21:18.763 --> 00:21:20.180
A heat exchanger
is just something

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:22.880
that transfers heat from
one thing to another

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:24.200
if you were wondering.

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:27.320
And so there's a lot of--

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:32.090
solar thermal is way less
efficient than solar PV,

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:32.990
photovoltaics.

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:35.780
But it's also
significantly cheaper.

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:37.760
I don't have numbers
for you for that,

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:42.680
but you don't want to
think of solar thermal

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:44.870
as only being a small
scale technology

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:48.300
because there are large scale
applications of it as well.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:50.162
This is sort of like
the smallest case,

00:21:50.162 --> 00:21:50.870
you can think of.

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:57.470
It would be like having one
of these for your household

00:21:57.470 --> 00:22:02.240
to heat water for showering
or something like that.

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:04.670
And then you have
larger scale versions

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:10.730
of this too, which is the Luz
Project in the Mojave Desert.

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:13.768
And it's the largest solar
thermal electric installation

00:22:13.768 --> 00:22:14.310
in the world.

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:19.090
It has 350 megawatt peak output.

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:23.420
And you can see,
like, this picture.

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:25.590
Those are three people
standing next to it.

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:28.690
So this thing is massive.

00:22:28.690 --> 00:22:30.590
It's on the order of kilometers.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:38.510
Yeah, and the way this works
is it's similar to this

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:44.990
except you have your pipes
that have water in them.

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:47.110
But then you have these
concentrating troughs

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:58.390
that are focusing all of
the sunlight onto the pipes.

00:22:58.390 --> 00:23:03.620
And so with the way
this technology works

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:05.795
is you heat up
water to the point

00:23:05.795 --> 00:23:07.420
where it turns into
steam, and then you

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:09.100
have the steam power a turbine.

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:14.450
And then you produce
electricity using that.

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:15.294
Yeah.

00:23:15.294 --> 00:23:17.743
AUDIENCE: I taught they
used oil in the pipes.

00:23:17.743 --> 00:23:18.660
PROFESSOR: Wait, what?

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:19.810
AUDIENCE: They use
oil in the pipes.

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:20.301
PROFESSOR: Oh, they use oil?

00:23:20.301 --> 00:23:22.756
AUDIENCE: Yeah, they use
oil through the pipes

00:23:22.756 --> 00:23:27.180
to bring to the
general community.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:28.770
PROFESSOR: Did you do research?

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:32.064
AUDIENCE: I did solar energy
consulting this past summer

00:23:32.064 --> 00:23:32.880
at DLA.

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:37.223
So we looked at the energy
problems with CSP power plants

00:23:37.223 --> 00:23:38.636
in the US.

00:23:38.636 --> 00:23:40.050
It was really cool.

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:42.590
AUDIENCE: What does
it [INAUDIBLE]??

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:46.568
The thermal part I get, but
what would the electrical be?

00:23:46.568 --> 00:23:48.110
AUDIENCE: Those
power plants are used

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:50.920
for [INAUDIBLE] generation.

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:51.610
PROFESSOR: So--

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:52.520
AUDIENCE: Oh, I see.

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:53.020
[INAUDIBLE]

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:55.853
PROFESSOR: So these
generate electricity.

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:01.120
Yeah.

00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:02.358
Does that make sense?

00:24:02.358 --> 00:24:05.212
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:05.212 --> 00:24:07.420
PROFESSOR: So you have steam,
which powers a turbine.

00:24:07.420 --> 00:24:09.250
And that turbine
generates electricity.

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:11.875
And so not only do you have--

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.140
yeah, like, you're not
doing something simple

00:24:19.140 --> 00:24:20.430
like heating water anymore.

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:22.233
You're also using
the same technology,

00:24:22.233 --> 00:24:24.150
but now you're generating
electricity from it.

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:29.910
And I thought I would
throw this in just for fun.

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:32.340
This was another cool--

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.380
like, this is a
project that I was

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:37.590
working on last year in Brazil.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.725
And it's a really low
cost solar water heater.

00:24:43.523 --> 00:24:44.940
But the reason I
threw this in was

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:48.300
because a way a lot of these
lower cost cheaper ones work

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:51.710
is they work based
off of a thermosiphon.

00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:58.290
And so basically what
happens is when you have--

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:06.830
what happens is in
a system like this,

00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:12.205
you'll have a system like this
that's connected to a tank.

00:25:24.770 --> 00:25:26.330
And so water heats up in this.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:32.240
And as water heats up,
like, if you remember from--

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:34.550
I guess you learned
this in middle school.

00:25:34.550 --> 00:25:37.530
Hot air rises-- like,
things like that.

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:38.970
So hot water also rises.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:46.260
So you got a flow of water
towards the top of this thing.

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:49.310
So what happens is if you
connect this to a reservoir,

00:25:49.310 --> 00:25:54.110
you'll get an--

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:56.943
you basically get a flow
caused just by the temperature

00:25:56.943 --> 00:25:58.110
differentiable in the water.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:01.700
So hot water will rise,
flow into this reservoir,

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:03.770
and it'll get displaced
by cold water.

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:07.040
And so you're regulating
the temperature

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:09.120
and heating all of the
water consistently,

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:12.445
which is pretty cool because
with a system that's based off

00:26:12.445 --> 00:26:13.820
of a thermosiphon,
you don't need

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:17.000
to throw in a pump or
something like that to extract

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:18.240
the water from your--

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:18.740
yes.

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:20.435
AUDIENCE: What's
the [INAUDIBLE]??

00:26:20.435 --> 00:26:21.800
Is it a heat siphon?

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:23.510
PROFESSOR: Yeah, it's
just a heat siphon.

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:31.690
But yeah, so it's just a passive
way of doing the same flow.

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:36.860
I'm going to go into--

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:40.220
are there any questions on
any of what I've talked about?

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:43.590
I know I did, like, a really,
really cursory job if you

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:46.610
transfer earlier, but that's
because we're going to go more

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:53.600
into it when we were talking
about photovoltaics and more

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:56.330
next week when you're
going into conductance

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:58.490
and convection for cooking.

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:00.980
But are there any
other questions

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:06.530
that you guys have about
what we just talked about?

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:12.170
OK, so we're going to
move on to photovoltaics.

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:17.120
I'm going to try to explain
to you how solar cells work.

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:19.280
I got-- a lot of
this information

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:23.960
was taken from this
website called pveducation.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:26.120
It actually used to
be called PVCDROM,

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:31.190
which I thought was pretty funny
because it's a website now.

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.860
So I guess it's been
around for a while.

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:35.580
Are you familiar with this?

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:37.340
Yeah, OK, .

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:40.955
It's a great resource for a lot
of information that you might--

00:27:40.955 --> 00:27:42.830
it'll answer a lot of
the questions you have,

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:45.650
and it does a pretty
good job of keeping

00:27:45.650 --> 00:27:48.980
its level, like, basic
enough for you to understand

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:52.370
plus being mathy enough for
you to actually understand

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:57.660
what's going on too if
you're interested in that.

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:01.160
So let's start by saying
you should go on that

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:05.360
and read up on more of
it want if you have time

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:08.690
and you are inclined to learn
more about how photovoltaics

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:09.450
work.

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:13.250
But anyway, the
temperature of the sun--

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:16.065
does anyone know what the
temperature of the sun is?

00:28:16.065 --> 00:28:16.565
About--

00:28:23.532 --> 00:28:26.107
AUDIENCE: Isn't it 10,000
Kelvin or something?

00:28:26.107 --> 00:28:27.440
PROFESSOR: That's a little high.

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:30.620
AUDIENCE: Like 1 million Kelvin?

00:28:30.620 --> 00:28:33.395
PROFESSOR: That's
a little higher.

00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:41.220
It's less than-- it's
about 6,000 Kelvin.

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:42.180
AUDIENCE: 60?

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:48.310
PROFESSOR: 6,000.

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:52.110
Well-- so to be fair,
when you have the--

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:55.000
so you have the sun, and
it's kind of like that.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:58.240
You have the core, where
there's fusion going on.

00:28:58.240 --> 00:29:02.540
And I think the core temperature
is maybe like 20 million

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:05.528
Kelvin or something like that.

00:29:05.528 --> 00:29:07.353
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:07.353 --> 00:29:08.770
PROFESSOR: So
something like that.

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:10.145
It's something
ridiculously high.

00:29:10.145 --> 00:29:13.150
I might be off by an
order or two of magnitude

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:14.330
in some direction.

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:17.660
But it's not really
that important.

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:19.420
But what happens is
most of that energy

00:29:19.420 --> 00:29:24.580
is blocked off by the sort of
dense hydrogen around here.

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:29.282
And then you have
the very end of--

00:29:29.282 --> 00:29:31.240
you have, like, the outer
rim of the sun, which

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:34.660
is at about 6,000 Kelvin,
which is what's radiating

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:38.350
most of the heat to the Earth.

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:41.320
And so that's where we get
all of our energy from.

00:29:43.870 --> 00:29:48.790
It's kind of cool to look at
a graph of what the energy--

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:54.640
of what the
irradiance looks like.

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:56.980
So this is what wavelengths--

00:29:56.980 --> 00:29:59.020
so this is a weird graph.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:02.410
The red, green, and blue
are three different types

00:30:02.410 --> 00:30:06.460
of lighting, and the
purple is what the sun is.

00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:09.100
So you can ignore
the other three,

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:10.840
but you should
look at the purple

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:15.280
to see what wavelengths of
light the sun outputs at.

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:21.120
It's interesting because you
can see that-- this is also

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:23.670
on a log scale.

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:29.100
So you can see that
the bulk of the light

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:34.960
is in the visible spectrum,
which is cool because--

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:35.460
yeah.

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:53.090
And to measure how much energy
is being outputted by the sun,

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:55.250
you can just integrate
under this curve

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:59.210
to measure the entire thing.

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:05.030
Do you guys know what the
energy from a single photon

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:06.740
is based off of the wavelength?

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:08.460
Do you remember the
equations for that?

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:10.340
So you can calculate--

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:12.358
did someone say it?

00:31:12.358 --> 00:31:13.588
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:13.588 --> 00:31:14.255
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:31:14.255 --> 00:31:16.190
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:19.090
PROFESSOR: It's Planck's
constant times c over lambda.

00:31:19.090 --> 00:31:22.078
So you have this.

00:31:22.078 --> 00:31:23.620
You don't need to
know this equation,

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:26.170
but it'll give you
a good sense of how

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:28.030
you can derive
how much energy is

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:32.380
coming from a photon or a wave.

00:31:32.380 --> 00:31:35.020
So this is Planck's constant.

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:38.950
This is the speed of light,
and this is the wavelength.

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:43.690
And so what's
important from this

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:46.520
is that the lower
the wavelength,

00:31:46.520 --> 00:31:50.550
the more energy you have.

00:31:50.550 --> 00:31:53.640
So yeah, your blue
light is going

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:55.920
to have more energy
than your red light.

00:31:58.660 --> 00:31:59.535
Does that make sense?

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:03.570
Cool.

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:06.710
So you have this.

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:07.890
So you have your sun.

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:08.970
It's outputting.

00:32:11.530 --> 00:32:14.590
So you have the sun,
which you're looking at,

00:32:14.590 --> 00:32:18.010
as radiating a bunch of
electromagnetic waves

00:32:18.010 --> 00:32:22.870
in the form of, I guess, photons
to the Earth, which is here.

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:32.255
And then we were trying
to look at what happens.

00:32:32.255 --> 00:32:37.520
Like, if we're trying to follow
the path of this radiation,

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:42.110
we can see that not all of
it reaches the Earth or not

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:44.292
all of it that reaches
the Earth makes it

00:32:44.292 --> 00:32:46.250
to the surface of the
Earth because a lot of it

00:32:46.250 --> 00:32:50.700
is absorbed by our atmosphere.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:55.790
And so this is a kind of
graphic that'll show you

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:59.360
where a lot of energy is lost.

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:03.040
So only about 70% of it is
actually directed to the Earth.

00:33:08.410 --> 00:33:12.310
3% is scattered
to space, and 18%

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:16.630
is absorbed by the atmosphere.

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:21.160
Now we're going to go into
how a solar cell works.

00:33:24.790 --> 00:33:28.930
So yeah, OK.

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:34.310
So this is also a
little bit complicated.

00:33:38.530 --> 00:33:41.010
So you have a solar cell,
which looks like this

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:42.630
if you haven't seen one before.

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:43.620
They're super fragile.

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:46.798
We can pass them around.

00:33:46.798 --> 00:33:47.715
These are broken ones.

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:56.430
Evergreen solar was kind enough
to donate 200 of these duds

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:59.470
for this lab and
for our projects.

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:03.105
So we'll be playing with them
and wiring them up today.

00:34:06.330 --> 00:34:13.512
So are you-- what happened?

00:34:13.512 --> 00:34:15.429
AUDIENCE: I wanted to
put my thumbprint on it,

00:34:15.429 --> 00:34:17.185
and I broke the slide.

00:34:17.185 --> 00:34:19.090
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
they're super fragile.

00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:19.900
You can see why--

00:34:19.900 --> 00:34:22.067
it's one of the reasons why
working with solar cells

00:34:22.067 --> 00:34:24.340
is such a pain because
they're so easy to break.

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:27.949
Like, you have to solder them
together to make them work.

00:34:27.949 --> 00:34:31.159
So it's kind of difficult.
We'll get into that later.

00:34:31.159 --> 00:34:33.409
So how do they work?

00:34:33.409 --> 00:34:48.040
So you basically have two
layers in your solar cell

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:50.350
as is described in this drawing.

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:54.699
And what happens is you
have a photon coming in.

00:34:54.699 --> 00:35:00.250
And for one, they're
a semiconductor.

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:05.230
So who can tell me what
a semiconductor is?

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:11.580
OK, have-- yes.

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:12.930
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:19.990
And you have to have
exactly the right bandgap

00:35:19.990 --> 00:35:24.350
in between the two so that
when a photon hits it,

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:28.160
when a photon hits on it, it
activates exactly what you

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:30.877
need to [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:30.877 --> 00:35:31.960
PROFESSOR: That's correct.

00:35:31.960 --> 00:35:36.200
Yeah, did everyone
understand that?

00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:42.400
OK, so basically, what
happens is in a semiconductor,

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:46.360
you have things in a
pretty ordered pattern.

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:57.640
So you'll have, like,
atom, something like that.

00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:00.000
And so they're all bent
but bound in the slightest.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:03.570
And so what happens is they
don't have free electrons right

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:06.750
now, but you can get a
free electron from them

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:11.310
if you excite the electron
with enough energy.

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:14.670
And so if you have a photon
coming in and it has the right

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:18.450
amount of energy to excite an
electron out of one of these

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:23.042
bonds, then the
valence electron--

00:36:23.042 --> 00:36:24.000
or out of the lattice--

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:29.320
the electron becomes excited.

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:32.430
And you then get an
electron hole pair.

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:34.800
And this is all a
gross simplification

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:38.040
of how this works because
a lot of this is--

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:41.730
like, I mean, you can't
really just think of--

00:36:41.730 --> 00:36:43.560
you can think of
electrons as particles

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:45.750
that are moving
across a circuit,

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:47.370
but that's not
actually how it works.

00:36:47.370 --> 00:36:50.520
But for this, you're
going to assume

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:53.310
this simplification makes
sense to try to conceptually

00:36:53.310 --> 00:36:54.560
understand how it works.

00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:57.150
So what happens is have
a photon hitting this.

00:36:57.150 --> 00:37:01.800
So you get this
electron hole pair.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:06.850
And you can think of a hole as
where the electron was before.

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:10.290
And so now there's this space
where this electron was.

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:13.020
And so it's kind
of a positive area

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:19.710
because there's less electron.

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:21.640
Like, there's no
electron there anymore.

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:24.000
So you can think of it kind
of like a bubble in a fluid

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:24.670
almost.

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:32.430
And so this electron wants
to go back into this bubble,

00:37:32.430 --> 00:37:36.540
and this bubble is free to move
as well just like the electron

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:38.760
is, free to move
through the structure

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:41.560
now because it
can go through it.

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:43.830
And so what happens is if you--

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:47.730
but the thing is that you're
using two different types

00:37:47.730 --> 00:37:53.400
of silicon, and one of them
is an n type, and one of them

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:54.120
is a p type.

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:56.850
And the n and p just stand
for negative and positive.

00:37:56.850 --> 00:38:00.580
And so the other thing
is you can dope silicon.

00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:05.010
And what that means is
you put impurities in it,

00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:08.520
and that creates a
permanent electric field

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:09.810
inside the silicon.

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:14.100
And so one of your sides is
more positive, and one of them

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:17.110
is more negative.

00:38:17.110 --> 00:38:26.453
So when you create this
electron hole pair here,

00:38:26.453 --> 00:38:27.870
they're going to
want to be pulled

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:38.770
towards opposite directions
just based off of drift.

00:38:38.770 --> 00:38:43.590
And so once they get
pulled to either side,

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:46.560
if you create a circuit and
you put a load on this thing,

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:49.980
then you can have the
electron flow through here

00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:51.820
and go back into the holes here.

00:38:51.820 --> 00:38:55.410
Then it'll close the circuit
again and combine with the--

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:05.110
yeah, so that's a
very simplified way

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:08.420
of how photovoltaics work.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:11.242
Does that make sense to people?

00:39:11.242 --> 00:39:12.700
Are there any
questions about that?

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:17.300
AUDIENCE: So the
positive [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:36.328 --> 00:39:38.370
PROFESSOR: No, the n type
should be the negative.

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:39.083
AUDIENCE: Huh?

00:39:39.083 --> 00:39:40.500
PROFESSOR: The n
type is negative.

00:39:40.500 --> 00:39:43.980
AUDIENCE: There's a really
good animation [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:47.304
that shows the two different
types of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:47.304 --> 00:39:48.042
electron.

00:39:48.042 --> 00:39:49.000
PROFESSOR: That's true.

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:52.800
We could actually just watch it.

00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:54.210
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:58.440 --> 00:39:59.905
PROFESSOR: I had it opening.

00:39:59.905 --> 00:40:01.360
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:06.700 --> 00:40:09.710
PROFESSOR: OK, yeah.

00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:27.190
Anyway, so that's basically
how photovoltaics work.

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:28.780
You get-- yes.

00:40:28.780 --> 00:40:31.240
AUDIENCE: Doesn't sunlight
