00:46:19.610 --> 00:46:25.000
Yeah, so let's look at some
of the financial tools that

00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:31.450
are available in order
to finance solar.

00:46:31.450 --> 00:46:40.090
So you can do the four sort
of big ones are microfinance,

00:46:40.090 --> 00:46:46.720
a fee for service, income
generation, or smaller systems

00:46:46.720 --> 00:46:49.180
and then see how for
microfinance, you

00:46:49.180 --> 00:46:52.000
have smaller payments over
a longer period of time.

00:46:52.000 --> 00:46:56.980
For fee for service, you have
a company that owns a PV system

00:46:56.980 --> 00:47:01.810
and a household that pays
the electric bill for it.

00:47:01.810 --> 00:47:04.030
For income generation,
which we saw,

00:47:04.030 --> 00:47:06.310
I think, in the lighting
lecture last week,

00:47:06.310 --> 00:47:08.920
we saw how people were
using solar panels

00:47:08.920 --> 00:47:10.600
to generate income.

00:47:10.600 --> 00:47:16.660
You use a system to improve
your income stream so you can--

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:19.180
with something like this,
you'll have one person

00:47:19.180 --> 00:47:22.390
with a lot of solar panels who
will charge a lot of batteries

00:47:22.390 --> 00:47:25.600
and rent them out
as electricity.

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:31.870
And then for
smaller systems, you

00:47:31.870 --> 00:47:33.225
can scale the technology down.

00:47:33.225 --> 00:47:35.350
So you're not using the
giant solar panels anymore,

00:47:35.350 --> 00:47:37.780
but you're using
smaller solar panels

00:47:37.780 --> 00:47:41.650
to power lantern or something
like that, for example.

00:47:45.340 --> 00:47:48.850
Yeah, and so that
was the bulk of what

00:47:48.850 --> 00:47:55.600
I wanted to talk about
today about solar cells.

00:47:55.600 --> 00:47:57.700
I guess one of the
interesting things

00:47:57.700 --> 00:48:00.610
to note about them
is that it's weird

00:48:00.610 --> 00:48:04.570
because their technology
is that's very much--

00:48:07.780 --> 00:48:09.520
there's a quote
from Harish Hande,

00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:12.220
who is the founder
of SELCO, that's

00:48:12.220 --> 00:48:15.880
solar is expensive for the rich
and affordable for the poor.

00:48:15.880 --> 00:48:19.120
It's the rich who
need subsidies.

00:48:19.120 --> 00:48:22.720
And it's this weird
thing because electricity

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:30.112
for the amount that people need
in the developing world for,

00:48:30.112 --> 00:48:31.570
the amount of energy
that they need

00:48:31.570 --> 00:48:34.670
there, solar is a very
cheap solution for that

00:48:34.670 --> 00:48:39.025
whereas the amount of
electricity that we're

00:48:39.025 --> 00:48:40.900
using at like at the
scales we're looking at,

00:48:40.900 --> 00:48:42.760
solar is a very
expensive option compared

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:45.340
to other sorts of electricity.

00:48:45.340 --> 00:48:46.720
So it's pretty important.

00:48:46.720 --> 00:48:49.510
Like, it's pretty
important to think about--

00:48:49.510 --> 00:48:51.760
or it's important to
think of solar is not

00:48:51.760 --> 00:48:56.860
an expensive technology per
se because I think in the US,

00:48:56.860 --> 00:49:01.870
at least, we have that bias
as solar being expensive.

00:49:01.870 --> 00:49:04.660
And SELCO makes
profit because they

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:06.980
sell all their solar panels
without subsidies at all.

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:10.230
So I thought that
was pretty cool.

